[{"order":1,"type":"heading","text":"Generic Constraints and Default Types in TypeScript"},{"order":2,"type":"paragraph","text":"TypeScript generics provide a way to make components work with any data type while maintaining type safety. However, sometimes you want to restrict the types a generic can accept, or provide a sensible fallback if no type is explicitly passed. This tutorial covers generic constraints and default types."},{"order":3,"type":"subheading","text":"Understanding Generic Constraints"},{"order":4,"type":"paragraph","text":"By default, a type parameter can be anything. A generic constraint allows you to limit the types that a generic type can accept by using the "extends" keyword. This ensures that the type has certain properties or methods."},{"order":5,"type":"code","text":"interface HasLength {
  length: number;
}

function logLength<T extends HasLength>(item: T): T {
  console.log(item.length);
  return item;
}

logLength("Hello TypeScript"); // Valid
logLength([1, 2, 3]);          // Valid
// logLength(123);           // Error: number does not have a length property"},{"order":6,"type":"subheading","text":"Using Keyof with Generic Constraints"},{"order":7,"type":"paragraph","text":"A common pattern in TypeScript is constraining a type parameter to be a key of another object using the "keyof" operator. This is extremely useful for safely accessing object properties dynamically."},{"order":8,"type":"code","text":"function getProperty<T, K extends keyof T>(obj: T, key: K) {
  return obj[key];
}

const user = { id: 1, name: "Alice" };

const name = getProperty(user, "name"); // Valid
// const age = getProperty(user, "age"); // Error: "age" is not a key of user"},{"order":9,"type":"subheading","text":"Default Generic Types"},{"order":10,"type":"paragraph","text":"Just like function parameters can have default values, TypeScript generics can specify a default type using the "=" operator. If the consumer of the generic does not supply a type argument, the default type is used."},{"order":11,"type":"code","text":"interface ApiResponse<T = any> {
  data: T;
  status: number;
  error?: string;
}

const defaultResponse: ApiResponse = {
  data: { anything: "goes" },
  status: 200
};

const typedResponse: ApiResponse<{ userId: number }> = {
  data: { userId: 42 },
  status: 200
};"},{"order":12,"type":"subheading","text":"Combining Constraints and Defaults"},{"order":13,"type":"paragraph","text":"You can combine both features in a single generic parameter. The default type must satisfy the constraint specified for that generic parameter."},{"order":14,"type":"code","text":"interface BaseUser {
  id: number;
}

function createUser<T extends BaseUser = { id: 0; name: string }>(userData: T): T {
  return userData;
}

const user1 = createUser({ id: 1, name: "Bob" }); // Uses default type fallback shape if needed"},{"order":15,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Use constraints (extends) to guarantee that generic types contain specific properties or structures."},{"order":16,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Use keyof constraints to ensure type safety when dealing with object keys and dynamic property lookup."},{"order":17,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Use default types (= Type) to simplify API usage by providing sensible fallbacks when type arguments are omitted."}]
